1
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:08,350
Hi. Did Sheldon change the Wi-Fi password again?

2
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:11,110
Yeah, it's "Penny already eats our food.

3
00:00:11,110 --> 00:00:12,560
She can pay for Wi-Fi."

4
00:00:14,470 --> 00:00:15,600
No spaces.

5
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:16,840
Okay.

6
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,320
If you can't get me to stop eating your food,

7
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:21,070
what makes you think you can get me to stop using your Wi-Fi?

8
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:23,620
I believe that you're capable of great change.

9
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:27,030
Like when I finally got you to stop saying "Valentimes Day""

10
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:29,950
You want to hear something weird?

11
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:30,530
Sure.

12
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:32,390
In the year 2000,

13
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:36,800
Pope John Paul II was named an honorary Harlem Globetrotter.

14
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:41,740
What are you talking about?

15
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:44,130
You asked Penny if she wanted to hear something weird.

16
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:47,770
Yeah, because I have something weird to tell her.

17
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,230
I thought it was a game.

18
00:00:52,270 --> 00:00:54,020
What's yours?

19
00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:56,170
There's this guy, Jimmy Speckerman,

20
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:58,410
who used to torment me in high school.

21
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:00,380
He sent me a message through Facebook.

22
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:02,580
He's in town and wants to have drinks.

23
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:05,820
Okay, Penny, if it were a game, here are your choices.

24
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:07,510
An e-mail from an old acquaintance,

25
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,940
or the head of one of the largest religious institutions

26
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:13,680
in the world slam dunking to "Sweet Georgia Brown." Pick.

27
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:19,410
Just do it, 'cause he's not gonna let it go.

28
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:21,570
Basketball Pope.

29
00:01:21,570 --> 00:01:23,030
And that's how it's done.

30
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:27,000
What are you gonna do about your bully?

31
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:28,490
- Are you gonna see him? - I don't know.

32
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:31,660
Is this the fellow who peed in your Hawaiian Punch?

33
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,570
No, that was a different guy.

34
00:01:34,570 --> 00:01:36,840
Was he the one who wedgied you so hard,

35
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:38,260
your testicle reascended,

36
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:39,960
and you spent your whole Christmas break

37
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:41,470
waiting for it to come back down?

38
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:47,490
No, that was a different, different guy.

39
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,740
Was he the one who used your head to open a nut?

40
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:54,270
No.

41
00:01:54,270 --> 00:01:55,490
Oh, oh, oh.

42
00:01:55,490 --> 00:01:58,470
Was he the one who made you eat your arm hair?

43
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:00,790
No, but, actually,

44
00:02:00,790 --> 00:02:02,530
that was this guy's sister.

45
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:04,990
All right, well, what do you think he wants?

46
00:02:04,990 --> 00:02:06,250
I don't know.

47
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:08,130
You know, the holidays are just around the corner.

48
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:09,510
Maybe he wants to see if he can lodge

49
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:11,060
the other testicle up there.

50
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:14,550
I told you. That was a different guy.

51
00:02:14,550 --> 00:02:16,700
Hmm. That's too bad. We could have spent

52
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:18,900
New Year's Eve waiting for the ball to drop.

53
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:53,680
It's 2:00 a.m. What are you doing up?

54
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:58,270
Nobel Prize acceptance ceremony streaming live from Stockholm.

55
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:00,730
Sure. You want to see

56
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:02,600
what all the scientists are wearing this year.

57
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:05,340
Look at these men.

58
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:08,750
They've managed to win the top science prize in the world

59
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:11,610
with no more understanding of the quantum underpinnings

60
00:03:11,610 --> 00:03:13,710
of the expansion of the early universe

61
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:15,680
than God gave a goose.

62
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:19,060
You should pay attention, Leonard.

63
00:03:19,110 --> 00:03:20,340
Someday this could be you up there.

64
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:23,590
Thanks.

65
00:03:23,590 --> 00:03:24,980
So, what's got you up?

66
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:26,980
Did you have a bad clam?

67
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:29,650
I didn't have clams.

68
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:31,690
I don't watch you 24 hours a day.

69
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:32,600
I don't know what you do.

70
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:37,130
It's this Jimmy Speckerman thing.

71
00:03:37,130 --> 00:03:39,880
I can't decide if I should agree to see him or not.

72
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:41,100
Of course that might be

73
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:42,480
because the last time I ran into him,

74
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:44,700
he made me floss with my own shoelaces.

75
00:03:46,330 --> 00:03:48,290
Wear loafers.

76
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:51,880
Look at Dr. Saul Perlmutter up there,

77
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:53,610
clutching that Nobel Prize.

78
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:54,450
What's the matter, Saul?

79
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:55,980
You afraid someone's going to steal it?

80
00:03:56,030 --> 00:03:58,520
Like you stole Einstein's cosmological constant?

81
00:04:01,050 --> 00:04:01,680
You know what?

82
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,200
I am tired of living in fear of this guy.

83
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,980
I'm gonna go see him and finally say

84
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,620
all the things I should have said in high school.

85
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:10,800
You know, "Pick on someone your own size"

86
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,860
"You did not have sex with my mother."

87
00:04:14,910 --> 00:04:17,950
And "Yes, I do know why I'm hitting myself."

88
00:04:19,350 --> 00:04:21,510
Oh, now Perlmutter's shaking the king's hand.

89
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:22,970
Yeah, check for your watch, Gustaf.

90
00:04:22,970 --> 00:04:24,140
He might have lifted it.

91
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:28,770
I love this dress.

92
00:04:28,770 --> 00:04:30,500
How come I never see you wear it?

93
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:32,580
'Cause when I wear it, it's a shirt.

94
00:04:35,290 --> 00:04:37,050
So, what's Howard doing tonight?

95
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:40,100
Oh, they all went with Leonard to confront his childhood bully.

96
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:41,360
Oh, terrific.

97
00:04:41,370 --> 00:04:43,890
High school quarterback against four mathletes.

98
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:46,400
When Leonard gets back,

99
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:48,040
I'd love to check his serotonin levels.

100
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:51,330
Do you think he'd let me draw a syringe full of his blood?

101
00:04:51,790 --> 00:04:53,400
Hmm, he's not crazy about needles,

102
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:54,610
but if you get him to go jogging,

103
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:56,240
it'll just pour out of his nose.

104
00:04:57,510 --> 00:04:58,720
I don't think I can meet the girl

105
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,140
who was always mean to me.

106
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:02,440
Tammy Bodnick.

107
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:04,080
One time while I was in gym class,

108
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:05,550
she stole all my clothes

109
00:05:05,570 --> 00:05:08,070
and left an elf costume in my locker.

110
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:10,690
Oh, that's awful.

111
00:05:10,690 --> 00:05:12,400
Worst part was, it was too big.

112
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:15,450
That's nothing.

113
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:18,720
In ninth grade, the girls put Rogaine in my hand lotion.

114
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:22,060
Within six months, the nicknames began to fly.

115
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:25,290
I think the one that hurt the most was Gorilla Fingers Fowler.

116
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,690
Wow. You poor thing.

117
00:05:30,690 --> 00:05:32,580
- What about you? - Oh.

118
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,720
I don't know. I guess my school was a nice place.

119
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:35,850
We didn't really have bullies.

120
00:05:35,850 --> 00:05:38,720
Come on, no one ever gave anyone mean nicknames

121
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:41,360
or picked on them or put gum in their hairy knuckles

122
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:43,600
so the school nurse had to use peanut butter to get it out?

123
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:46,500
No, we weren't really like that.

124
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:48,050
I mean, look, we played pranks on each other,

125
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:49,400
but it was never mean.

126
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:51,130
Like, okay, this one girl, Kathy Geiger,

127
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:52,670
got really good grades, so we blindfolded her,

128
00:05:52,670 --> 00:05:54,730
tied her up and left her in a cornfield overnight.

129
00:05:57,810 --> 00:05:59,410
Oh, my God, that's awful.

130
00:05:59,410 --> 00:06:00,840
No, it was funny. Everyone laughed.

131
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:04,200
Did Kathy Geiger laugh?

132
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:05,900
Uh, probably. It's hard to say.

133
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:08,040
She kind of had an ear of corn in her mouth.

134
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:11,650
Who would have thought

135
00:06:11,650 --> 00:06:14,360
Fuzzy Fingers Fowler is best friends with a bully?

136
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:17,530
What? I was not a bully.

137
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:19,470
Kind of sounds like you were.

138
00:06:19,470 --> 00:06:21,140
And maybe a felon.

139
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:26,320
Shh. That's how you wind up in a cornfield.

140
00:06:32,470 --> 00:06:33,710
Is that him over there?

141
00:06:34,700 --> 00:06:35,520
No.

142
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:37,220
How about that guy?

143
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:38,760
He looks like he'd hate you.

144
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:41,360
You know, I can really do this by myself.

145
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:42,920
Hey, we're here to support you, buddy.

146
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:44,310
No, you're not. You're here to see

147
00:06:44,330 --> 00:06:46,400
if I get my underwear pulled over my head.

148
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:49,650
You wore underwear? You fool.

149
00:06:51,190 --> 00:06:53,540
So, have you figured out what you're going to say to him?

150
00:06:53,930 --> 00:06:56,530
You bet. I am going to make him apologize

151
00:06:56,530 --> 00:06:58,980
for all the crap he pulled on me in school.

152
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:00,430
That's quite a list.

153
00:07:00,430 --> 00:07:02,280
I can't read your handwriting.

154
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:03,440
What's that word?

155
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:04,830
"Scrotum."

156
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:07,570
What's that one?

157
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:09,990
Uh, "Stapled."

158
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:12,990
Leonard.

159
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:17,600
Oh, hi.

160
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,200
Holy crap, man, it's good to see you.

161
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:22,920
Yeah. You, too. Uh, Jimmy, this is

162
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:24,730
Sheldon and Raj and Howard.

163
00:07:24,730 --> 00:07:26,420
Hi. Fellas... Hey, can I get a beer?

164
00:07:27,030 --> 00:07:28,110
Wow. Look at you.

165
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:29,820
Little Leonard Hofstadter.

166
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:31,840
I hear you're a big-time scientist now.

167
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:34,120
And there's the first zinger. Ouch.

168
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:37,340
I'm doing okay, I guess.

169
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:38,710
Okay? Come on, I read online

170
00:07:38,760 --> 00:07:40,450
you're a physicist at a university,

171
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:41,890
you won some medal.

172
00:07:41,890 --> 00:07:43,550
The Newcomb medal.

173
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:45,110
Yeah, congratulations.

174
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:46,610
Congratulations?

175
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:48,320
The Newcomb medal? Oh, please.

176
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:49,550
That's the scientific equivalent

177
00:07:49,570 --> 00:07:52,020
of a smiley face sticker on your homework.

178
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:54,840
From what I read, it sounded like a big deal.

179
00:07:54,840 --> 00:07:56,900
Oh, good Lord, are we going to stand here

180
00:07:56,920 --> 00:07:59,240
and listen to him tear Leonard apart like this?

181
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:02,240
Hey, I won a Newcomb medal, too.

182
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:03,500
My point.

183
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:06,910
You should have seen this guy back in the day.

184
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:09,650
Huh? He was so little, he could fit in just about anywhere.

185
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:11,380
Lockers, trash cans.

186
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:14,650
Oh, man, how did you get inside that backpack?

187
00:08:16,690 --> 00:08:18,200
Oh, I can't take all the credit.

188
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:20,340
- You helped a lot. - Yeah.

189
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:22,450
We were practically a comedy team.

190
00:08:22,450 --> 00:08:24,210
Like the Black Death and Europe.

191
00:08:26,670 --> 00:08:29,730
Jimmy, I'm kind of curious why you wanted to see me.

192
00:08:29,730 --> 00:08:31,280
Okay, here it is.

193
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:33,000
I have this great money-making idea.

194
00:08:33,030 --> 00:08:35,130
I just need a gear head to get it to the finish line.

195
00:08:35,130 --> 00:08:36,990
Technically, Howard's the gear head.

196
00:08:37,010 --> 00:08:39,150
Leonard's just a dime store laser jockey.

197
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:41,230
What's the idea?

198
00:08:41,230 --> 00:08:43,440
This is just between us, right?

199
00:08:43,470 --> 00:08:44,630
- Right. - Okay.

200
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:45,910
What do you think about a pair of glasses

201
00:08:45,950 --> 00:08:48,760
that makes any movie you want into 3D?

202
00:08:49,350 --> 00:08:50,940
That sounds amazing.

203
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:52,970
First movie I'm watching-- Annie.

204
00:08:56,080 --> 00:08:57,880
How exactly would these glasses work?

205
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:00,500
How the hell should I know? That's why I need a nerd.

206
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:04,400
I don't think something like that's even possible.

207
00:09:04,460 --> 00:09:06,370
Aw, come on, you can figure it out.

208
00:09:06,410 --> 00:09:08,100
You're like the smartest guy I've ever known.

209
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:11,070
The smartest...?! All right,

210
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:14,440
you know, I may not have a firm grasp on sarcasm,

211
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:17,340
but even I know that was a doozy.

212
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:20,370
Leonard, you can't live in fear of this man forever.

213
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:21,990
Sheldon, I got this.

214
00:09:21,990 --> 00:09:23,530
You clearly don't.

215
00:09:23,530 --> 00:09:25,890
What my spineless friend lacks the courage to say

216
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:27,960
is you're a terrible person

217
00:09:27,960 --> 00:09:30,290
who took advantage of his tiny size,

218
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:32,430
his uncoordinated nature and

219
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:34,540
his congenital lack of masculinity.

220
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:35,050
Sheldon...

221
00:09:35,070 --> 00:09:37,140
Leonard, I platonically love you, man,

222
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:38,980
but face it, you're a mess.

223
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:43,220
I don't understand.

224
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:47,520
I think what he's trying to say is that maybe in high school

225
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:49,090
you picked on me a little bit.

226
00:09:49,090 --> 00:09:50,920
A little bit?

227
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:54,170
The man super glued Hershey's Kisses to your nipples.

228
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:58,460
That's funny because those aren't the kind of kisses

229
00:09:58,460 --> 00:09:59,960
you want on your nipples.

230
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:02,670
What is that?

231
00:10:02,670 --> 00:10:05,770
This is a list of your heinous acts against Leonard.

232
00:10:05,770 --> 00:10:07,500
One of which is certainly the cause

233
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:09,770
of him wetting his bed well into his teens.

234
00:10:11,490 --> 00:10:14,340
14 is not... Aw, never mind.

235
00:10:16,570 --> 00:10:18,220
What's this word?

236
00:10:19,050 --> 00:10:20,220
"Nancy".

237
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:25,190
You called me Nancy for three years.

238
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:28,640
You really need to work on your penmanship.

239
00:10:30,410 --> 00:10:32,090
Oh, man, I...

240
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:34,400
I don't know what to say.

241
00:10:34,870 --> 00:10:37,090
I always thought we were just having some fun.

242
00:10:37,090 --> 00:10:39,340
It wasn't fun for me.

243
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:41,370
You're being too kind, Leonard.

244
00:10:41,370 --> 00:10:43,190
You ruined him.

245
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:45,880
Come on, guys.

246
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:48,010
That was pretty badass, dude.

247
00:10:48,010 --> 00:10:49,750
I help the weak.

248
00:10:49,750 --> 00:10:52,100
It's yet another way I'm exactly like Batman.

249
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:56,520
Hey, for the record, Jimmy wasn't the reason I wet the bed.

250
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:59,500
That one has my mother written all over it.

251
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:04,960
Anyway, I'm really sorry

252
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:07,320
I made fun of your stutter in high school.

253
00:11:07,890 --> 00:11:08,990
You're doing great.

254
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:15,190
Oh, God, just finish the sentence.

255
00:11:19,470 --> 00:11:22,970
Okay, well, I'm sorry you feel that way. Bye.

256
00:11:23,690 --> 00:11:25,710
No one wants to hear my apologies.

257
00:11:25,710 --> 00:11:27,710
I think your mistake is doing it over the phone.

258
00:11:27,710 --> 00:11:29,950
If they could look into your eyes, they'd melt.

259
00:11:32,410 --> 00:11:34,520
Penny, it doesn't matter what you did in the past.

260
00:11:34,520 --> 00:11:36,180
You're a good person now.

261
00:11:36,190 --> 00:11:37,680
That's easy for you to say.

262
00:11:37,690 --> 00:11:40,380
You weren't just called a "B-b-b-b-bitch".

263
00:11:42,810 --> 00:11:45,630
Perhaps you could assuage your guilt through altruism.

264
00:11:48,970 --> 00:11:51,090
Which word's tripping you up?

265
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:53,980
"Assuage", or "altruism"?

266
00:11:55,260 --> 00:11:56,440
Both.

267
00:11:57,470 --> 00:12:00,210
You'll feel better by doing something nice for someone.

268
00:12:00,210 --> 00:12:02,260
- I actually knew that.  - I never doubted you.

269
00:12:04,150 --> 00:12:07,530
Every other week I serve at a soup kitchen downtown.

270
00:12:07,530 --> 00:12:09,250
Ooh, I can't do that.

271
00:12:09,250 --> 00:12:12,750
If I stand over a steaming pot, my hair just goes boing!

272
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:15,020
What else could I do?

273
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:16,460
There's Habitat for Humanity --

274
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:17,890
building houses for the poor.

275
00:12:17,890 --> 00:12:19,140
Okay, come on, I don't even have my own house --

276
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:21,550
I'm going to build one for someone else?

277
00:12:22,730 --> 00:12:24,800
How about donating some of your clothes?

278
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:27,670
Oh, my God, that's perfect.

279
00:12:27,670 --> 00:12:29,970
'Cause I have so many clothesI don't wear,

280
00:12:29,970 --> 00:12:30,950
and they're just taking up space,

281
00:12:30,950 --> 00:12:32,060
and I go shopping to buy more stuff

282
00:12:32,060 --> 00:12:33,240
and I have no place to put it.

283
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:34,710
This will totally fix that.

284
00:12:34,710 --> 00:12:36,080
What about helping people?

285
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:37,960
And helping people.

286
00:12:40,830 --> 00:12:42,370
Here's your cocoa.

287
00:12:42,370 --> 00:12:44,990
- Oh, half and half instead of whole milk?  - Yes.

288
00:12:44,990 --> 00:12:47,390
- Heated to precisely 183 degrees?  - Yes.

289
00:12:47,390 --> 00:12:49,290
Seven little marshmallows, no more no less?

290
00:12:50,030 --> 00:12:51,780
You got one for good luck.

291
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:54,260
I'll get it.

292
00:12:55,470 --> 00:12:56,730
One for good luck.

293
00:12:56,730 --> 00:12:58,910
Must be the kind of math they do at Princeton.

294
00:13:04,540 --> 00:13:06,130
What are you doing here?

295
00:13:06,130 --> 00:13:10,510
I want to apologize for...stapling your balls and...

296
00:13:11,980 --> 00:13:13,980
...throwing you naked in the girls' locker room,

297
00:13:13,980 --> 00:13:16,580
stuffing that parrot down your pants.

298
00:13:18,220 --> 00:13:19,720
What's this word?

299
00:13:20,250 --> 00:13:23,470
- "Laxative".  - Oh, right.

300
00:13:23,470 --> 00:13:25,160
Junior prom.

301
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:28,450
That was not cool, man.

302
00:13:29,230 --> 00:13:31,660
I am so, so sorry.

303
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:32,970
Really?

304
00:13:32,970 --> 00:13:35,740
Yeah. I just hope you can forgive me.

305
00:13:38,170 --> 00:13:39,170
Yeah.

306
00:13:39,170 --> 00:13:40,460
Sure, I guess.

307
00:13:40,460 --> 00:13:42,770
You're a beautiful guy.

308
00:13:42,770 --> 00:13:45,580
Well, thanks, Jimmy.

309
00:13:45,580 --> 00:13:47,620
Okay, I got to go.

310
00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:49,080
Are you okay to drive?

311
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:51,280
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I drive better drunk.

312
00:13:51,290 --> 00:13:53,890
You know, it makes you pay attention.

313
00:13:55,960 --> 00:13:58,730
No, no, no, come on in. I'll make you a cup of coffee.

314
00:13:58,730 --> 00:14:00,060
- I wouldn't be imposing? - No.

315
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:01,270
Yes.

316
00:14:03,670 --> 00:14:05,440
Sheldon, we can't let him drive.

317
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:08,000
Then take away his keys and make him wander the streets

318
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:08,880
with the other drunks.

319
00:14:12,490 --> 00:14:14,470
You remember Sheldon and Raj and Howard.

320
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:16,040
Not really, no.

321
00:14:17,310 --> 00:14:18,750
It's funny, huh, Leonard?

322
00:14:18,750 --> 00:14:21,650
Back in school, I was the winner and you were the loser.

323
00:14:21,650 --> 00:14:22,720
And now we're reversed.

324
00:14:23,310 --> 00:14:25,150
You're the winner.

325
00:14:25,150 --> 00:14:28,040
You'd think a winner could make a decent cup of cocoa.

326
00:14:30,670 --> 00:14:33,060
- You mind if I use your bathroom? - Yeah, just back there.

327
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:37,500
How about that?

328
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:40,950
After all these years, your big bad high school bully

329
00:14:40,950 --> 00:14:42,450
finally apologizes.

330
00:14:42,450 --> 00:14:45,240
Yeah. It kind of rekindles your faith

331
00:14:45,240 --> 00:14:47,420
in the basic goodness of people.

332
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:49,980
You know what would be nice?

333
00:14:49,980 --> 00:14:50,920
What's that?

334
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:52,990
As a symbolic gesture

335
00:14:52,990 --> 00:14:55,640
to all the bullies who've tormented us for years,

336
00:14:55,640 --> 00:14:59,840
we open our home to Jimmy and once he's asleep we kill him.

337
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:08,820
I said it would be nice-- I didn't say we should do it.

338
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:14,650
I feel just like Mother Teresa.

339
00:15:15,210 --> 00:15:16,440
Except for the virgin part.

340
00:15:16,440 --> 00:15:18,340
That ship sailed a long time ago.

341
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:23,270
I think Mother Teresa would have washed the clothes first.

342
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:26,030
Yeah, well, I bet her laundry room

343
00:15:26,030 --> 00:15:28,090
wasn't down five flights of stairs.

344
00:15:29,050 --> 00:15:31,950
You know, giving really is better than receiving.

345
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:35,730
I used to think it was such a cliche, but it seems to be the...

346
00:15:35,730 --> 00:15:39,200
Look at these cute jeans someone just threw away.

347
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:41,660
Donated.

348
00:15:44,070 --> 00:15:47,520
Yes, to a poor waitress who loves a boot cut.

349
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:50,010
Penny.

350
00:15:50,010 --> 00:15:51,940
Come on, they would be so cute on me...

351
00:15:51,940 --> 00:15:54,140
They go great with this sweater!

352
00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:56,280
I don't think Mother Teresa...

353
00:15:56,280 --> 00:15:57,950
Oh, that is adorable.

354
00:16:10,500 --> 00:16:11,840
- Leonard. - Yeah.

355
00:16:11,840 --> 00:16:13,380
In case it comes up again,

356
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:16,590
this right here is an imposition.

357
00:16:18,920 --> 00:16:20,000
What was I supposed to do?

358
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,810
He needed a place to sleep it off.

359
00:16:21,810 --> 00:16:23,300
You're soft.

360
00:16:23,310 --> 00:16:25,570
This world's going to chew you up and spit you out.

361
00:16:29,990 --> 00:16:31,680
When did I have tacos?

362
00:16:33,150 --> 00:16:34,650
Morning, Jimmy.

363
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:36,340
Oh, there it is, tacos.

364
00:16:39,920 --> 00:16:41,370
Man, I tied one on.

365
00:16:41,370 --> 00:16:42,610
Yeah, you did.

366
00:16:42,610 --> 00:16:46,190
So, uh, listen, it was great to see you again.

367
00:16:46,190 --> 00:16:48,230
And thanks for the apology.

368
00:16:48,230 --> 00:16:49,890
What apology?

369
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:53,540
For all the crappy stuff you did to me in high school.

370
00:16:53,540 --> 00:16:55,550
Geez, you're still harping on that?

371
00:16:55,550 --> 00:16:57,240
What a puss.

372
00:16:58,240 --> 00:16:59,770
That's my French toast.

373
00:16:59,770 --> 00:17:00,590
It's good.

374
00:17:01,660 --> 00:17:04,120
You really know your way around a kitchen, Nancy.

375
00:17:06,820 --> 00:17:08,370
I'm not going to say I told you so,

376
00:17:08,370 --> 00:17:10,170
but we could have killed him.

377
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:15,370
I might kill him right now.

378
00:17:15,370 --> 00:17:17,910
The Dark Knight has your back.

379
00:17:18,330 --> 00:17:19,440
He's scared, but he has your back.

380
00:17:21,190 --> 00:17:23,120
Okay, Jimmy, it's time for you to go.

381
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:24,500
Yeah, all right, let me just finish this.

382
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:27,330
No, you're done. I want you out of my apartment right now.

383
00:17:27,330 --> 00:17:29,030
Well said, Boy Wonder.

384
00:17:31,700 --> 00:17:32,900
Or what?

385
00:17:33,350 --> 00:17:34,420
Don't answer that. It's a trick question.

386
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:35,830
I speak from experience.

387
00:17:39,890 --> 00:17:41,460
I'm not afraid of you anymore, Jimmy.

388
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:43,530
Now get out!

389
00:17:48,190 --> 00:17:50,250
You did it, Leonard, you stood up to your bully.

390
00:17:50,250 --> 00:17:51,780
Yeah, I feel pretty good about myself.

391
00:17:55,250 --> 00:17:56,520
You think we can outrun him?

392
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,260
I don't need to outrun him, I just need to outrun you.

393
00:18:05,900 --> 00:18:07,120
I don't feel good about this.

394
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:09,590
Then sit in the car and keep it running.

395
00:18:10,190 --> 00:18:13,110
You were right-- a whole new load.

396
00:18:15,900 --> 00:18:18,530
Come on, yoga top. Mama needs a new yoga top.

397
00:18:18,530 --> 00:18:21,800
Check it out, Bernadette, suede boots, your size.

398
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:23,870
God, they're cute.

399
00:18:23,870 --> 00:18:26,070
Oh, why did they have to be cute?

400
00:18:26,070 --> 00:18:29,030
Wait, wait, wait, guys, just hang on.

401
00:18:29,030 --> 00:18:30,480
What is it, the fuzz?

402
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:35,590
Look at us.

403
00:18:35,590 --> 00:18:36,970
What are we doing?

404
00:18:36,970 --> 00:18:39,850
I was gleefully following you to a life of crime,

405
00:18:39,850 --> 00:18:41,890
looking forward to the day we might be cell mates.

406
00:18:46,950 --> 00:18:48,250
I don't know about Bernadette.

407
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:53,410
You know, this is wrong. Let's put everything back.

408
00:18:54,940 --> 00:18:55,510
Here.

409
00:19:05,810 --> 00:19:08,640
It's okay, I serve soup to poor people!

